Z||000001||West Coast Fiero||12-29-2001||11:58 PM||||the pressure plate will work, however the clutch disk will not as it is a different spline count and size, and nor will the throwout bearing due to different design<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://clubs.hemmings.com/fieroswest/WestCoast300.gif">  <BR>Eric Nelson<BR>Technician,<BR>West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111<p>[This message has been edited by West Coast Fiero (edited 12-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroPerformance||12-30-2001||12:26 AM||||Yeah, clutch and pressure plate will work. Throwout bearing needs to match the tranny though. I would go with the GM pressure plate because it isn't as tall as the type you can get at autozone. I've heard horror stories of the pressure plate getting hung up on the inside of the Isuzu bell housing. I am getting ready to put the V6 clutch and pressure plate in my Isuzu<P>-Charlie<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/newsigfperformance.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||jelly2m8||12-30-2001||12:37 AM||||I've personally seen 2 issues with the 4 spd clutch hanging up in the webbing and release bearing shaft bosses of the isuzu.<P>jimmybpei says he had no problems using the getrag clutch in a isuzu.<BR>you might want to order the getrag clutch.<P> You will need to use the isuzu release bearing though.<P> Not disputing WCF, but the 4 spd clutch disks I used did fit the input shaft on the isuzus .<P> I did a reverse swap for a guy once, isuzu to a 4 spd, and the isuzu disk worked on the 4 spd input shaft.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg">   <P>[This message has been edited by jelly2m8 (edited 12-30-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by jelly2m8 (edited 12-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||West Coast Fiero||12-30-2001||01:00 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by jelly2m8:<BR><B>I've personally seen 2 issues with the 4 spd clutch hanging up in the webbing and release bearing shaft bosses of the isuzu.<P>jimmybpei says he had no problems using the getrag clutch in a isuzu.<BR>you might want to order the getrag clutch.<P> You will need to use the isuzu release bearing though.<P> Not disputing WCF, but the 4 spd clutch disks I used did fit the input shaft on the isuzus .<P> I did a reverse swap for a guy once, isuzu to a 4 spd, and the isuzu disk worked on the 4 spd input shaft.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ah, perhaps it was the Getrag I was thinking of, my bad<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://clubs.hemmings.com/fieroswest/WestCoast300.gif"> <BR>Eric Nelson<BR>Technician,<BR>West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fierotech||12-30-2001||02:08 AM||||All three tranny's have the same input shaft diameter and # of splines. (1"x14)<BR>The getrag clutch will definitely work in an Isuzu tranny with the Isuzu TO bearing, as FieroPerformance said.<BR>Now...here's a question for everyone. According to Centerforce's webpage, the 90-93 Grand Prix with a 3.1L used a 9 3/4" disc. I can't verify that, heck, I didn't know the 90-93 GP's came with a 3.1 & a stick. Ours are only 9 1/8". Flywheel has to be the same diameter, or at least interchangeable if the pressure plate bolt circle is different(for internally balanced motors). If it's not the goofy pull-type they used on the DOHC 3.4L's, then I don't see why it wouldn't work. Anyone tried (or willing to try) it?<P>Jeremy B.||||reg||1
Z||000006||ig88vsbobafett||12-30-2001||02:47 AM||||Yeah what i was wonering was Would it be a Strait swap for the CLuch to go From the Muncie 4 speed to the isuzu 5 speed (I know i have the change the Cable?? and the slave cyclinder i dont have the $$ for a new cluch right now and my trannny went||||reg||1
Z||000007||West Coast Fiero||12-30-2001||02:57 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ig88vsbobafett:<BR><B>Yeah what i was wonering was Would it be a Strait swap for the CLuch to go From the Muncie 4 speed to the isuzu 5 speed (I know i have the change the Cable?? and the slave cyclinder i dont have the $$ for a new cluch right now and my trannny went</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>this is really bugging me that I can't seem to truly recall - I know when dealing with all the clutch swap I have dealt with, that there was one clutch disk that would not slide over the input shaft of the trans - thats it, tearing open all the inventory untill I'm satisfied by being proven wrong or right here, I just want to be certain....looks like its going to be an all-nighter =-)<P> as far as the slave, you <I>might</I> be able to get away with retaining the one you have, but there are different part numbers so I will have to check that out as well<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://clubs.hemmings.com/fieroswest/WestCoast300.gif"> <BR>Eric Nelson<BR>Technician,<BR>West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000008||West Coast Fiero||12-30-2001||03:00 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fierotech:<BR><B>All three tranny's have the same input shaft diameter and # of splines. (1"x14)<BR>The getrag clutch will definitely work in an Isuzu tranny with the Isuzu TO bearing, as FieroPerformance said.<BR>Now...here's a question for everyone. According to Centerforce's webpage, the 90-93 Grand Prix with a 3.1L used a 9 3/4" disc. I can't verify that, heck, I didn't know the 90-93 GP's came with a 3.1 & a stick. Ours are only 9 1/8". Flywheel has to be the same diameter, or at least interchangeable if the pressure plate bolt circle is different(for internally balanced motors). If it's not the goofy pull-type they used on the DOHC 3.4L's, then I don't see why it wouldn't work. Anyone tried (or willing to try) it?<P>Jeremy B.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>with the clutches we supply, the max diameter we have been able to get away with has been 9 11/16"<P> God only knows how many different clutches from different GM cars we can get away with putting on a Fiero<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://clubs.hemmings.com/fieroswest/WestCoast300.gif"> <BR>Eric Nelson<BR>Technician,<BR>West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000009||Fierotech||12-30-2001||03:04 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ig88vsbobafett:<BR><B>Yeah what i was wonering was Would it be a Strait swap for the CLuch to go From the Muncie 4 speed to the isuzu 5 speed (I know i have the change the Cable?? and the slave cyclinder i dont have the $$ for a new cluch right now and my trannny went</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>If you're going from the 4speed to an Isuzu, you're going to have to change the shifter, both cables, the slave cylinder, and at least the throwout bearing, and you're going to loose your backup lights. Not to mention needing all the brackets, etc, for the Isuzu. <BR>I can say for sure that the Getrag clutch will work in an Isuzu; can't say for sure that the 4speed clutch will. It should; the pressure plates for the aftermarket 4speeds & Getrags are physically the same. But, see Jelly2m8's comments above. I don't have any experience with the 4speed clutch in an Isuzu.<BR>But...honestly, I would hate to think about going thru all that trouble, and recycling the clutch, unless you JUST put it in.<P>Hope this is some help...<BR>Jeremy B.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Fierotech (edited 12-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Fierotech||12-30-2001||03:12 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by West Coast Fiero:<BR><B> this is really bugging me that I can't seem to truly recall - I know when dealing with all the clutch swap I have dealt with, that there was one clutch disk that would not slide over the input shaft of the trans -<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Eric---<P>Only thing I can think of is the DOHC clutch. It's got a 24 spline shaft, as far as I recall. All three Fiero ones are definitely 1"x14.<P>Jeremy B.<P>BTW...what was the limiting factor on the 9 11/16" max clutch diameter?||||reg||1
Z||000011||West Coast Fiero||12-30-2001||03:30 AM||||any more on the diameter and there would be no room to mount a pressure plate that would clear the inner bellhousing<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://clubs.hemmings.com/fieroswest/WestCoast300.gif"> <BR>Eric Nelson<BR>Technician,<BR>West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000012||Fierotech||12-30-2001||03:33 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by West Coast Fiero:<BR><B>any more on the diameter and there would be no room to mount a pressure plate that would clear the inner bellhousing<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I've got a grinder.<BR>j/k<BR>Thanks for the info.<P>Jeremy B.||||reg||1
Z||000013||West Coast Fiero||12-30-2001||03:40 AM||||HAHAHA||||reg||1
Z||000014||fieroluke||12-30-2001||08:13 AM||||I've just swapped an Isuzu into my V6, replacing a 4spd.<P>I used a V6 aftermarket clutch and pressure plate, but the Isuzu TO bearing. I also kept the 4spd slave cylinder (85 model -&gt; old style) as well as master (also old style). Works great, clutch very easy to depress. Jacked up rear wheels, put car in gear, pressed clutch: wheels don't rotate, so the clutch really releases. I then backed out of our driveway (45 degree angle), and that worked too, so the clutch doesn't seem to slip (no smell either).<P>I did use the Isuzu shifter cables and shifter mechanism, although the old mechanism probably also would have worked, if you remove the reverse lockout mechanism.<P>As for reverse, my car had been an automatic before I changed to 4spd manual, so the wiring for the backup lights was already in place, I just had to extend the wiring from the old connector to the new one. I still haven't found a connector though, so I had to <BR>crimp new weatherpack terminals to the wiring and insert the terminals directly into the backup switch connector, fastening and securing them with (ack!) tape. If anyone has a donor wiring harness, I'd really appreciate  <BR>a 2 pin weatherpack connector... ;-)<P>------------------<BR>Visit my website: <A HREF="http://www.fieros.de" TARGET=_blank>www.fieros.de</A>  for lots of technical and other Fiero related information<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fieros.de/jpg/mycars.jpg"> ||||reg||14
